# ============================================================================
# Pynchy Secrets (.env)
# ============================================================================
# Copy this file to .env in the project root and fill in your values:
#   cp config-examples/.env.EXAMPLE .env
#
# Non-secret config stays in data/personalization/pynchy.toml. This file is for
# API keys, tokens, and passwords only. Uses __ as the nested delimiter.
#
# .env is already in .gitignore — never commit it.
# ============================================================================

# ─── API Credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SECRETS__ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...
# SECRETS__OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-...
# Host-side repository operations only. This never grants agent CLI access.
# SECRETS__GH_TOKEN=ghp_...

# ─── Tool Requirements ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Names declared by selected tools in data/personalization/pynchy.toml.
# Runtime-backed tools receive them only in their process unless the tool opts
# into workspace exposure. Use Proton Pass host-process materialization in
# production instead of this local-development file.

# LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_...
# LINEAR_TEAM_KEY=SYN
# GITHUB_TOKEN=github_pat_...
# PYNCHY_PROTON_BRIDGE_USERNAME=you@example.com
# PYNCHY_PROTON_BRIDGE_PASSWORD_COMMAND=/path/to/read-bridge-app-password

# ─── Gateway (LiteLLM) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Env vars referenced by os.environ/ in data/personalization/litellm.yaml are auto-forwarded
# into the LiteLLM container at startup. Add keys here, reference in YAML.

# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-...
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-api03-...

# LiteLLM -> Phoenix tracing. Required when personalized litellm.yaml enables
# callbacks: ["arize_phoenix"]. Pynchy defaults LITELLM_OTEL_V2=true and
# OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=SPAN_AND_EVENT so Phoenix
# can serve as conversation-history source of truth.
# PHOENIX_COLLECTOR_HTTP_ENDPOINT=https://phoenix.example.com/v1/traces
# PHOENIX_PROJECT_NAME=pynchy
# PHOENIX_API_KEY=only-needed-for-phoenix-cloud

# GATEWAY__MASTER_KEY=your-master-key
# GATEWAY__UI_PASSWORD=your-secure-password

# ─── Slack Channel Plugin ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SLACK__BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
# SLACK__APP_TOKEN=xapp-...

# ─── Slack MCP Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Browser tokens for korotovsky/slack-mcp-server (read-only Slack access).
# The upstream server requires these exact names. Tokens can be extracted
# manually (see docs/integrations/slack-mcp.md) or
# automatically via the slack-token-extractor plugin.

# SLACK_MCP_XOXC_TOKEN=xoxc-...
# SLACK_MCP_XOXD_TOKEN=xoxd-...


# No credentials needed for the slack-token-extractor plugin — it uses
# persistent browser sessions instead. See docs/integrations/slack-mcp.md.

# ─── CalDAV (Calendar) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One entry per server defined in [caldav.servers.*] in config.toml.
# Name must match the password_env field in the corresponding server config.
#
# Use single underscores — double underscores (CALDAV__X__Y) are treated as
# nested config paths by pydantic-settings and would shadow CalDAV model fields.

# CALDAV_WORK_PASSWORD=your-app-password
# CALDAV_PERSONAL_PASSWORD=your-app-password
